Copyright status
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Public domain everywhere this application is distributed.

  * France (droit d'auteur, vie de l'auteur + 70 ans): entered the public domain in
    1975. Jules Verne was not "Mort pour la France", so the +30-year extension of
    art. L123-10 CPI does not apply — the extension that keeps Le Petit Prince under
    copyright in France to this day, and the reason this book is the example and that
    one is not.
  * United States (published before 1929): public domain, with no renewal question to
    ask.
  * Everywhere else on a life+70 or shorter term: 1975 or earlier.

The engravings are not bundled — the source text carries only Project Gutenberg's
`[Illustration: …]` markers, which the converter drops — but both illustrators are
likewise long in the public domain, so adding them later raises no new question.

