What the conversion changed
---------------------------

The prose is Verne's, and only the following was done to it. Every rule is declared
in `le-tour-du-monde-en-quatre-vingts-jours.toml`, under `[source]`, and applied by

    cargo run -p skribisto-skrib-format --example build_example -- convert \
        resources/examples/le_tour_du_monde_en_80_jours/le-tour-du-monde-en-quatre-vingts-jours.toml \
        <the stripped source text> \
        resources/examples/le_tour_du_monde_en_80_jours/le-tour-du-monde-en-quatre-vingts-jours.skrib

  * The Hetzel title page, the volume's own table of contents, the printer's mark and
    the transcriber's list of corrections were dropped. None of them is the book, and
    the corrections list in particular quotes misprints that would otherwise be
    searchable as though Verne had written them.
  * `[Illustration: …]` markers were removed.
  * Paragraphs hard-wrapped at 72 columns by the transcription were rejoined into
    single paragraphs. The wrap is a property of a terminal, not of the prose, and
    Verne's italics span it.
  * The typewriter apostrophe (') became the typographic apostrophe (’).
  * The 37 chapter headings moved from the prose into the rows' titles, where the
    application shows and numbers them.
  * The one footnote in chapter IX became a real footnote row rather than a paragraph
    beginning "[1]".

The 1873 spelling is kept exactly as it stands — "collége", "très-sérieusement", "A"
without its accent, "Saville-row" for Savile Row. These are not typing errors and
must not be "fixed": the dictionary is set to fr-FR-x-classique so the spell-checker
does not underline the book's own orthography.

The converter checks all 37 chapter titles against the headings it finds in the
source, so a chapter landing one slot out is a hard error rather than thirty-seven
quietly mismatched synopses.


Editorial metadata added by the Skribisto project
-------------------------------------------------

The synopses, the story bible (21 characters, 17 places, 13 objects), the labels,
the tags and the point-of-view assignments are the work of the Skribisto project and
not of Jules Verne. They are declared in the TOML beside this file and applied by the
same tool's `enrich` subcommand, which asserts on every run that the prose it read
back is byte-identical to what it writes out.

They are editorial description of the novel, not part of it. Any error in them is the
project's.
